  "markdown": "## Overview\n\nCostco operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites based on the concept of offering low prices on a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products in a wide range of categories to produce high sales volumes and rapid inventory turnover [10-K line 00134]. The company buys most merchandise directly from suppliers, routing it through cross-docking consolidation points or directly to warehouses. This approach creates freight volume and handling efficiencies that lower distribution costs compared to traditional multiple-step channels [10-K line 00135]. Costco's warehouses are designed for efficiency in selling space, merchandise handling, and inventory control, with floor plans not emphasizing elaborate decoration. The company strictly controls entrances and exits and uses a membership format to reduce inventory losses (shrinkage) below those of typical retail operations [10-K line 00139].\n\nCostco competes globally with a wide array of wholesalers and retailers, including supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, gasoline stations, hard discounters, department and specialty stores, and operators selling single categories or narrow ranges of merchandise [10-K line 00190]. In the U.S., significant competitors include Walmart, Target, Kroger, and Amazon, as well as other warehouse clubs such as Sam’s Club and BJ’s Wholesale Club [10-K line 00190].\n\nKey performance indicators for Costco in 2026 include net sales of $297,247 million, membership fee revenue of $5,907 million, net income of $9,226 million, and a membership base of 84,100 thousand paid members [10-K line 00433, 00458, 00422, 00161].\n\n## Market Positioning and Growth Outlook\n\nCostco differentiates itself through its membership format, which reinforces member loyalty and provides continuing fee revenue. It also leverages private-label brands like Kirkland Signature that are perceived by members as high quality and offered at prices generally lower than national brands [10-K line 00192].\n\nGrowth catalysts for Costco include: membership growth reaching 84,100 thousand paid members in 2026 [10-K line 00161]; executive member penetration of 50.3% of total members [10-K line 00166]; comparable sales growth of 8% driven by increases in average ticket and shopping frequency [10-K line 00451]; digitally-enabled comparable sales increasing 21% during 2026 [10-K line 00452]; and the opening of 28 new warehouses, including three relocations, for a total of 25 net new warehouses [10-K line 00415].\n\n## Future Developments and Strategic Recommendations\n\nCostco's future development strategy focuses on continued expansion of its warehouse footprint and e-commerce capabilities. The company has reported strong performance in digital sales growth and is expanding globally with operations in the U.S., Canada, the U.K., Mexico, Korea, Taiwan, Japan, Australia, and China [10-K line 00130]. Additionally, Costco plans to maintain its focus on member satisfaction and loyalty through competitive pricing, high-quality private-label products, and strategic investments in warehouse infrastructure. The company's recent approval of a 13% increase in the quarterly cash dividend indicates confidence in future earnings and a commitment to returning value to shareholders [10-K line 00423].\n\nStrategic recommendations include continued investment in technology and logistics to support scalable e-commerce operations, enhancing member retention through loyalty programs, and exploring international markets for further expansion opportunities. The company's average warehouse space of approximately 148,000 square feet suggests potential for growth in both domestic and international markets [10-K line 00139].",
